## 2. The Convergence Argument

### 2.1 Definition

A **convergence argument** occurs when multiple people agree in their testimony about an empirical phenomenon, and then conclude that this phenomenon is real . In the philosophy of science, robustness arguments are convergence arguments where different experimental approaches display the same phenomenon. In political science, the Condorcet Jury Theorem is based on a convergence argument where majority decision-making is claimed to point to true propositions .

### 2.2 The Pragmatic Tradition: Peirce

C.S. Peirce, the founder of pragmatism, articulated the foundational insight of emergent convergence. He stated:

> "The opinion which is fated to be ultimately agreed to by all who investigate is what we mean by truth." 

Peirce believed that any two minds investigating a given question would tend eventually to arrive at the same answer, even if they used different methods and different pools of evidence . Moreover, this one answer that all would reach is, by definition, the true answer .

For Peirce, truth is not dependent on actual unanimity or an actual end to inquiry. Rather, truth is "the satisfaction which would ultimately be found if the inquiry were pushed to its ultimate and indefeasible issue" . A true belief is one that "has and will continue to hold up to sustained inquiry"—one that is "dependable in the face of all future challenges" .

### 2.3 The Discourse Tradition: Habermas

Jürgen Habermas developed a complementary framework in his theory of communicative action. He argues that truth claims are always already at stake in deliberative processes. He stresses the "conflictual and latently political character of truth and facts," while maintaining that only speech oriented toward understanding and the "unforced force of the better argument" can form the normative basis of political judgment and action .

For Habermas, truth functions not only "vertically" as a founding principle but also "horizontally" as that which connects all citizens in an ideal communication community, committing them to the validity claims inevitably raised in every speech act .

Habermas seeks to defend an "emphatical," "uncurtailed" conception of reason against both the post-modern critique of reason and against scientism . His theory of communicative action aims to clarify the normative foundations of critical theory and provide a framework for empirical social research .

